<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
    Entitlements for the hardened runtime, which notarization requires.

    This list is deliberately almost empty, and that is worth explaining, because
    the app does several things that look like they should need an entitlement
    and do not:

    - It spawns a Python interpreter it downloaded itself. Spawning an unsigned
      or ad-hoc-signed executable needs no entitlement; the hardened runtime
      constrains *this* process, and the child is a separate one that inherits
      none of it.
    - It sets PYTHONPATH, HF_HOME and friends for that child. Only
      DYLD_* variables in our *own* environment would need
      allow-dyld-environment-variables.
    - It reads and writes Documents and Movies. The app is not sandboxed, so
      that is governed by TCC prompts, not entitlements.
    - It makes network requests. Only a sandboxed app needs to declare that.

    Add com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation only if the app itself
    ever dlopens a library signed by someone else. Today nothing does, and
    granting it weakens the app for no gain.

    com.apple.developer.usernotifications.time-sensitive was tried and removed.
    It is what would let a render-finished notification through a Focus, and on
    the merits these qualify — but it is restricted: Apple must enable it for
    the App ID, and it only applies with a provisioning profile. Claiming it
    anyway would leave the code looking as though Focus were handled while the
    system quietly ignored it. Settings explains the Focus behaviour instead.
    -->
</dict>
</plist>
